{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>You shall do no unrighteousness in judgment.</b> This teaches that a judge who corrupts the judgment is called an oppressor, hated and abominable, condemned and an abomination — for oppression is called abomination, as it says ״כִּי תוֹעֲבַת ה׳ וגו׳ כֹּל עֹשֵׂה עָוֶל״ (Deuteronomy 25), and abomination is called חֵרֶם and שֶׁקֶץ, as it says ״וְלֹא תָבִיא תוֹעֵבָה אֶל בֵּיתֶךָ וְהָיִיתָ חֵרֶם כָּמֹהוּ שַׁקֵּץ תְּשַׁקְּצֶנּוּ וגו׳״ (ibid. 7)." "<b>You shall not favor the face of the poor.</b> That you should not say: this one is poor, and the rich man is obligated to support him — I will find for him in judgment, and he turns out to be supported in cleanliness (Sifra)." "<b>And you shall not glorify the face of the great.</b> That you should not say: this one is rich, this one is a son of great ones — how can I embarrass him and see his shame? There is punishment in the matter; therefore it says ״וְלֹא תֶהְדַּר פְּנֵי גָדוֹל״." "<b>In righteousness you shall judge your fellow.</b> As it sounds. Another interpretation: judge your fellow toward merit (Shavuot 30)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:13.458703357Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}